---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import katex from 'katex';
import pipeline from '../../data/lightcurve/pipeline.py?raw';
import scanning from '../../data/lightcurve/scanning.py?raw';
import flare from '../../assets/lightcurve/flare-light-curve.png';
import s91 from '../../assets/lightcurve/sector-91.png';
import s92 from '../../assets/lightcurve/sector-92.png';

const centroid = katex.renderToString(
  String.raw`x_c = \frac{\sum_i x_i F_i}{\sum_i F_i}, \qquad y_c = \frac{\sum_i y_i F_i}{\sum_i F_i}`,
  { displayMode: true },
);
---

<Base
  title="Stellar Light Curves"
  description="Detecting stellar flares and variable stars in TESS data: pipeline, code, and light curves."
>
  <p class="crumb"><a href="/research/">← Research</a></p>
  <h1>Stellar Light Curves</h1>
  <p class="muted subtitle">Astrophysics · TESS data · October 2025–June 2026</p>

  <p>
    Working directly with data from NASA’s TESS satellite, I built a pipeline to
    detect and characterize stellar flares and to study how the brightness of
    stars changes over time. The work led to a co-authored research submission
    reporting a previously unreported variable star with frequent high-energy
    flares.
  </p>

  <figure class="plot wide">
    <Image src={flare} alt="Light curve: flux versus time showing several sharp flares around BTJD 3485–3489" widths={[800, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 64rem) 100vw, 64rem" />
    <figcaption>
      Aperture-masked light curve of TIC 1603615314 (TESS Sector 80). Sharp spikes
      are flare candidates; the pipeline then asks whether each one really comes
      from the target.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Is the flare on the star?</h2>
  <p>
    A spike in brightness is not enough: in TESS’s large pixels, light from a
    neighbouring star can masquerade as a flare. For each cadence the pipeline
    computes the flux-weighted centroid of the aperture,
  </p>
  <div class="display" set:html={centroid} />
  <p>
    and compares it between a pre-flare baseline and the flare peak. If the
    centroid shifts toward another source, the emission is likely contamination;
    if it stays on the target’s WCS-projected position, the flare is the
    target’s own.
  </p>

  <div class="pair">
    <figure class="plot">
      <Image src={s91} alt="Sector 91 light curve with two large flux spikes" widths={[600, 900]} sizes="(max-width: 40rem) 100vw, 30rem" />
      <figcaption>TESS Sector 91.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="plot">
      <Image src={s92} alt="Sector 92 light curve with large flux spikes" widths={[600, 900]} sizes="(max-width: 40rem) 100vw, 30rem" />
      <figcaption>TESS Sector 92.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Code</h2>
  <CodeTabs
    id="lc"
    files={[
      {
        name: 'pipeline.py',
        label: 'Flare detection',
        code: pipeline,
        summary:
          'For one target: build an aperture-masked light curve, detrend it and flag flares as runs of points above 3σ of the robust noise, compute flux-weighted centroids at every cadence, and test whether the centroid moves significantly, and toward or away from the target, during the strongest flare. Ends with a verdict and a diagnostic figure.',
        tools: ['lightkurve', 'astropy', 'numpy', 'matplotlib', 'TESS TPF', 'WCS'],
      },
      {
        name: 'scanning.py',
        label: 'NGC 2516 survey',
        code: scanning,
        summary:
          'The same idea scaled to a whole open cluster: select NGC 2516 members from Gaia DR3 by parallax and proper motion, cross-match to the TESS Input Catalog, download and stitch every light curve, extract variability features (amplitude, skewness, von Neumann ratio, Lomb–Scargle period, flare count), and rank stars with an Isolation Forest.',
        tools: ['astroquery', 'lightkurve', 'Gaia DR3', 'TESS TIC', 'pandas', 'scipy', 'scikit-learn'],
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .subtitle {
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }
  .plot {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .plot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: #fff;
  }
  .wide {
    --w: min(52rem, calc(100vw - 2rem));
    width: var(--w);
    margin-left: calc((100% - var(--w)) / 2);
  }
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .display {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
